# Distill — Cursor AI Rules

## Git Workflow

- The only two long-lived branches are `master` and `develop`.
- **Never touch `master` directly.** It is protected. All work flows through `develop`.
- For every new feature or bug fix, create a branch off `develop` using this naming convention:
  - New feature → `feature-short-description-here`
  - Bug fix → `fix-short-description-here`
  - Examples: `feature-file-preview-panel`, `fix-chunk-overlap-crash`
- Branch names must be lowercase, hyphen-separated, no spaces, no special characters.

## Feature Development Order

1. Cut a new branch from `develop`
2. Implement the feature
3. Write unit tests for that feature in `pytest` (see Testing Rules below)
4. Run all tests — every test must pass before proceeding
5. Push the branch to remote
6. Merge into `develop`
7. Delete the feature branch after merging

## Testing Rules

- All tests live in `backend/tests/`
- One test file per feature/module: `test_parser.py`, `test_chunker.py`, `test_embedder.py`, etc.
- Use `pytest` only — no other test framework
- Every new feature must have at least:
  - One happy-path test (expected input → expected output)
  - One edge-case test (empty input, bad file type, missing session, etc.)
- Tests must not make real LLM calls — mock the LLM service using `unittest.mock`
- Tests must not require a running ChromaDB instance — use in-memory or mocked clients
- All tests must pass with `pytest backend/tests/` before merging

## General Code Rules

- Follow the existing file and folder structure defined in the project spec
- After Each Feature update the Readme.md file
- Do not introduce new dependencies without updating `requirements.txt`
- Every function must have a docstring
- Keep functions small and single-purpose — if a function does two things, split it
- Never hardcode secrets, paths, or model names — use `config.py` and `.env`
